i='TCS-4054';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 1: integer feasibility and parameters; Section 5, page 31:12, two sublinear-exponent running-time questions and the alternative of ETH lower bounds.'
refs += [dict(id='solution',title='Tight Complexity Lower Bounds for Integer Linear Programming with Few Constraints',authors='Dušan Knop; Michał Pilipczuk; Marcin Wrochna',year=2019,url='https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.STACS.2019.44',pdf_url='https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/standa.zivny/publications/Knop19stacs-preprint.pdf',locator='Theorem 1: 0/1 coefficient matrices, n and ||b||∞ both O(m log m), and no 2^{o(m log m)} algorithm under ETH.'),dict(id='full',title='Tight complexity lower bounds for integer linear programming with few constraints — full version',authors='Dušan Knop; Michał Pilipczuk; Marcin Wrochna',year=2019,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.01296',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/1811.01296v3',locator='Version 3, 23 July 2019; Theorem 1 and Corollary 2. The latter also permits b∈{0,1}^m by allowing −1 entries in A.'),dict(id='journal',title='Tight Complexity Lower Bounds for Integer Linear Programming with Few Constraints',authors='Dušan Knop; Michał Pilipczuk; Marcin Wrochna',year=2020,url='https://doi.org/10.1145/3397484',locator='ACM Transactions on Computation Theory 12(3), Article 19. Publication corroborated by the authors’ institutional publication records; journal full text was not accessible.'),dict(id='updated',title='On the optimality of pseudo-polynomial algorithms for integer programming — journal version',authors=refs[0]['authors'],year=2023,url='https://doi.org/10.1007/s10107-022-01783-x',pdf_url='https://fedorvf.github.io/articles/2023/2023g.pdf',locator='Published online 14 February 2022; Mathematical Programming 198 (2023), pp. 561–593. Section 1.1, page 566, explicitly acknowledges that Knop et al. answered the conference lower-bound question.')]
notes=['Recovered standard-form feasibility with unbounded nonnegative integer variables and signed binary input, distinguishing row count from variable count.','Restored both runtime alternatives and their little-o quantifiers; stated deterministic ETH rather than silently asserting randomized or unconditional hardness.','Matched the 2019 Theorem 1 restricted instances to both proposed runtimes, with polynomial input-size overhead still allowed.','Read the 2019 revised full result and the original authors’ 2022-online/2023-print journal acknowledgment; marked the historical ETH tradeoff question resolved.']
save(i,dict(title='Sublinear row exponents for integer programming',formal='Historical question, resolved negatively under ETH: can integer-programming feasibility Ax=b, x∈ℤⁿ_{≥0}, for an arbitrary integer m×n matrix A and integer m-vector b, be decided by a deterministic algorithm in time (m n Δ)^{o(m)} B^{O(1)}, or even in time (m n Δ B)^{o(m)}? Here Δ=max{1,maxᵢⱼ|Aᵢⱼ|} and B=max{1,maxᵢ|bᵢ|}; both are numerical magnitudes, not bit lengths. Knop, Pilipczuk and Wrochna rule out both alternatives under ETH, even on instances with Δ=1 and n,B=O(m log m). The original source explicitly allowed a conditional lower bound as an answer.',definitions='''Inputs have m,n≥1, A∈ℤ^{m×n} and b∈ℤ^m, encoded by dimensions and the full signed binary lists of their entries. Let L be this total bit length. A YES instance has some vector x with n nonnegative integer entries satisfying every equality ∑ⱼ Aᵢⱼxⱼ=bᵢ; no upper bounds on x are supplied. A NO instance has no such vector. The task is exact decision of feasibility, with no objective vector and no approximation or real-valued relaxation. Ordinary deterministic Turing bit complexity is used, with worst-case time on the given parameters. Matrix coefficients may be negative; the resolution already holds for the nonnegative 0/1 subclass.

The first proposed bound means that one algorithm and constants C,c≥1 and a function g:ℕ→ℝ_{≥0} exist with g(m)/m→0 as m→∞, such that for every input its running time is at most C L^c (2mnΔ)^{g(m)} (2B)^c. The second means that one algorithm, constants C,c≥1 and such a function g exist with time at most C L^c (2mnΔB)^{g(m)} on every input. The exponent function and constants do not depend on n, A or b. Explicit factors 2 and clamping Δ,B at 1 remove degenerate zero or unit bases. A fixed polynomial factor in binary input length is allowed, making the lower-bound claim stronger and avoiding an implicit free input-reading convention. This does not permit an arbitrary factor depending on m. Any first-form bound is also a second-form bound after adding a constant to g; a second-form bound need not have only polynomial dependence on B.

ETH is the deterministic Exponential Time Hypothesis: satisfiability of Boolean 3-CNF formulas with N variables and encoding length M has no deterministic algorithm of time 2^{o(N)} M^{O(1)}. A 3-CNF is a conjunction of clauses with at most three literals each. The result is the implication from this hypothesis to nonexistence of the stated IP algorithms; it is not an unconditional time lower bound and does not replace ETH by its randomized analogue.

In the later theorem, the row and column parameters are named k and ℓ; they correspond to this card’s m and n. Theorem 1 restricts to A∈{0,1}^{m×n}, b∈ℕ^m, with n and ||b||∞ at most a universal constant times m log(m+2). It excludes time 2^{o(m log(m+2))} under ETH. On this restricted family, L is polynomial in m and each of the two proposed runtimes is 2^{o(m log(m+2))}; the extra L^c and polynomial B factor do not avoid the lower bound. Corollary 2 of the revised full text additionally puts b in {0,1}^m while allowing A entries in {−1,0,1}.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='The requested historical negative result is a proof that either proposed deterministic running-time bound implies failure of ETH, with the stated parameter dependencies. Theorem 1 establishes this already on the restricted 0/1-matrix family described above, and hence for the general problem. The result does not establish ETH itself or exclude bounded-error randomized algorithms under deterministic ETH alone.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The numerical parameters matter separately',text='With few equalities, integer feasibility admits pseudopolynomial algorithms whose exponent depends on the number of rows. The source asks whether additional dependence on columns and right-hand-side magnitudes could reduce that exponent below linear in the row count.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The later lower bound rules out both alternatives',text='Knop, Pilipczuk and Wrochna prove that 0/1 coefficient matrices still require 2^{Ω(m log m)} time in the ETH sense, even when the number of variables and right-hand-side magnitude are O(m log m). Both saved algorithmic alternatives would violate that lower bound on these instances.',citation='solution'),dict(title='Small right-hand sides also have a hard variant',text='The July 2019 full revision adds a corollary with right-hand-side entries only 0 or 1 and coefficient entries −1, 0 or 1. This is a further restricted hardness result, with the introduction of negative coefficients stated explicitly.',citation='full'),dict(title='The original authors record the resolution',text='Section 1.1 of the later Mathematical Programming article says that Knop et al. answered the conference version’s question about improving its lower bound. The article was published online in February 2022 and assigned to the 2023 volume.',citation='updated')],why='Identifies a sharp obstruction to improving pseudopolynomial integer-programming algorithms by trading among row count, column count and coefficient magnitudes. The hardness survives very small coefficients, so the cost is not solely caused by encoding enormous numbers.',progress=[dict(date='2018',text='The ESA paper asks for the two faster running times or strengthened conditional lower bounds.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2019',text='STACS Theorem 1 rules out both alternatives under ETH; the July full revision adds the binary-right-hand-side corollary.',citation='solution'),dict(date='2020',text='The tight lower-bound paper appears in ACM Transactions on Computation Theory.',citation='journal'),dict(date='2022-02-14',text='The original authors’ journal article acknowledges that the earlier lower-bound question has been answered; print volume year is 2023.',citation='updated')],references=refs,status='resolved',status_note='The historical request permits ETH-based lower bounds. Read the original two alternatives, STACS 2019 Theorem 1, arXiv v3 of 23 July 2019 and the original authors’ journal Section 1.1 acknowledging the answer. Checked exact restrictions n,B=O(m log m), Δ=1, and their implication for both runtimes. The 2020 tight-bound journal publication was corroborated by institutional records; its full text was inaccessible. Resolution is conditional on deterministic ETH, with no claim of independent proof verification.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Both saved alternatives are excluded by the same later theorem under the source’s accepted ETH framework. This is a historical resolved disposition, not a claim of unconditional hardness or a substitution by optimization, bounded variables or a randomized hypothesis.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A tight parameterized lower bound for a central general-purpose feasibility problem, separating genuine row-count cost from large coefficient encoding. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['The historical question concerns exact feasibility of integer equalities with nonnegative integer variables.','It asks whether the exponent on row count and numerical parameters can be made sublinear in the number of rows.','A 2019 theorem rules out both proposed runtimes under deterministic ETH, even for 0/1 matrices with few variables and moderate right-hand sides.','The original authors explicitly acknowledge that lower-bound resolution in their later journal article.','The card is therefore resolved conditionally, with the input representation and all parameter dependencies recorded.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','solution','full','updated'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/standa.zivny/powalgdo.html'])
p=DIRECTORY/'queue.json';q=json.loads(p.read_text());_,c=card(i)
